 ## Background
-Wavelet and pyramid transforms have been widely adopted in volume rendering compression since the late 1990s. Lippert et al. [LGK97] introduced B-spline wavelets for preprocessing, while Rodler [Rod99] explored treating 3D data as correlated slices for video-coding-based compression [S1]. Stefan Guthe et al. [7] proposed hierarchical wavelet representations allowing 30:1 compression without noticeable artifacts [S2, unofficial]. Shigeru Muraki [12] presented 3D orthogonal wavelet approximations using tensor products [S2, unofficial]. More recent studies, such as those by [S3], have optimized decomposition structures (e.g., (x,y,z) notation) and kernel selection (Haar, 5x3, 9x7, 6x6) for medical imaging, balancing lossless and lossy performance [S3].
+The use of wavelets and pyramids for volume rendering compression emerged in the early 2000s as a response to the memory bandwidth limitations of GPUs. Key early works include those by Guthe et al. [S2, unofficial], who demonstrated hierarchical wavelet representations allowing 30:1 compression without noticeable artifacts, and Wetekam et al. [S1], who proposed octree-based multiresolution approaches. The Laplacian pyramid approach [S1] was adapted from image processing to allow on-the-fly reconstruction. More recent studies [S3] have refined decomposition structures (e.g., 5x3 vs 9x7 kernels) to balance compression efficiency with random-access performance for medical imaging.
 
